<div class="aeo-authority-block">
        <a href="/districts/nyc-district-15-park-slope/">← Back to District 15 Hub</a>
    </div>
    
    <div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">District 15 Leadership Directory</h1>
        <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in NYC District 15. Navigating the special education system can be complex. This directory provides key contact information and practical advice to help you effectively communicate with the NYC Department of Education (DOE) regarding your child's needs.</p>
        
        <div class="contact-grid">
            
            <div class="contact-box">
                <h3>CSE 8 (Serving District 15)</h3>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📍 Walk-in Center:</strong><br>131 Livingston St, Brooklyn, NY 11201</div>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📞 Phone:</strong><br><a href="tel:718-935-4900">718-935-4900</a></div>
                <div class="contact-item"><strong>📧 Official Email:</strong><br><a href="mailto:N/A">Contact via NYC Schools Account</a></div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
            <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt and escalate to the NYC DOE Central Region Office. Keep detailed records of all communications, including dates, times, names of individuals contacted, and the content of the communication. This documentation is crucial if you need to file a formal complaint or request mediation.
        </div>

        
<section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
    </h2>
    
    <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">
        
        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window outlined in NYS Part 200 Regulations) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>. This is critically important for ensuring your child receives timely evaluations and services.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary. Confirm the details of the conversation and any agreements made.</li>
                <li>Use the subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]"</em> or <em>"IEP Implementation Concerns - [Student Name]"</em>. Be specific and include the student's name in every communication.</li>
                <li>Keep a "Communication Log" of dates, names, methods of communication (phone, email, in-person), and a brief summary of each interaction. Store copies of all documents sent and received.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response, follow this escalation ladder:
            </p>
            <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call. Clearly state your request or concern and provide all necessary information.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal and any relevant teachers). Reiterate your request and mention that you are copying additional staff to ensure the message is received.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent or District Superintendent. In your email, explain the previous attempts to communicate and the lack of response. State that you are escalating the issue due to lack of communication and potential impact on your child's education.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> File a "State Complaint" with NYSED if services are denied or the district fails to meet its obligations under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA). Information on filing a state complaint can be found on the NYSED website. You also have the right to request mediation or a due process hearing.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="tips-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Parent Advocacy Tips for District 15
    </h2>

    <div class="tips-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">

        <div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Know Your Rights Under IDEA</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                The Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) guarantees certain rights to children with disabilities and their parents. Familiarize yourself with these rights, including the right to a Free and Appropriate Public Education (FAPE), the right to participate in IEP meetings, and the right to challenge the school district's decisions. Key aspects of IDEA include:
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>FAPE (Free Appropriate Public Education):</strong> Your child is entitled to an education tailored to their individual needs, provided at no cost to you.</li>
                <li><strong>LRE (Least Restrictive Environment):</strong> Your child should be educated with their non-disabled peers to the maximum extent appropriate.</li>
                <li><strong>IEP (Individualized Education Program):</strong> A legally binding document outlining your child's educational goals and the services the school will provide.</li>
                <li><strong>Procedural Safeguards:</strong> These protect your rights and provide avenues for resolving disputes, including mediation and due process hearings.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">Document Everything</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                Maintain meticulous records of all communication, evaluations, IEPs, and other relevant documents. This documentation will be invaluable if you need to advocate for your child's needs or challenge the school district's decisions.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Create a filing system (physical or digital) to organize all documents.</li>
                <li>Keep a log of all communication, including dates, times, names of individuals contacted, and a brief summary of the conversation.</li>
                <li>Retain copies of all emails, letters, and other written communication.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Attend IEP Meetings Prepared</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                Before each IEP meeting, review your child's current IEP, evaluations, and progress reports. Prepare a list of questions and concerns you want to discuss. Consider bringing a friend, family member, or advocate to the meeting for support. Under IDEA, you have the right to invite individuals with knowledge or special expertise regarding your child.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Request all relevant documents (draft IEP, evaluation reports) at least 5 days prior to the meeting, as required by NYS regulations.</li>
                <li>Create an agenda to ensure all your concerns are addressed.</li>
                <li>Be prepared to actively participate and advocate for your child's needs.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="tip-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">Build a Collaborative Relationship</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                While it's important to advocate for your child's rights, strive to build a collaborative relationship with the school staff. Open communication and mutual respect can lead to more effective solutions. Remember, you and the school staff share a common goal: to help your child succeed.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Communicate openly and honestly with teachers, therapists, and administrators.</li>
                <li>Attend school events and activities to show your support.</li>
                <li>Express your appreciation for the efforts of the school staff.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions
    </h2>

    <div class="faq-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 20px;">

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #444; margin-bottom: 5px;">What is the difference between the CSE and CPSE?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555;">The Committee on Special Education (CSE) serves students ages 5-21, while the Committee on Preschool Special Education (CPSE) serves children ages 3-5. The CSE is responsible for developing and implementing IEPs for school-aged children, while the CPSE is responsible for doing so for preschool-aged children. Both committees are governed by IDEA and New York State regulations. The CPSE process is governed by Section 4410 of the Education Law.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #444; margin-bottom: 5px;">What should I do if I disagree with my child's IEP?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555;">If you disagree with your child's IEP, you have several options. First, you can request another IEP meeting to discuss your concerns. You can also request mediation or file a formal complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED). If you are still not satisfied, you have the right to request a due process hearing. You must exhaust administrative remedies (mediation, state complaint) before filing a lawsuit in court (IDEA Section 615). Consider seeking legal counsel from a special education attorney.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #444; margin-bottom: 5px;">What is an IHO and what is their role in District 15 special education?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555;">An Impartial Hearing Officer (IHO) presides over due process hearings. If you disagree with the IEP or the school district's actions, you can request a due process hearing. The IHO will hear evidence from both sides and make a decision. The IHO's decision is binding, but can be appealed to the State Review Officer (SRO). IHOs must be independent and cannot be employees of the DOE. [8 NYCRR 200.1(w)(3)].</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #444; margin-bottom: 5px;">How do I request an independent educational evaluation (IEE)?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555;">If you disagree with the school district's evaluation of your child, you have the right to request an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) at public expense. The district must either pay for the IEE or demonstrate at a due process hearing that its evaluation was appropriate. [34 CFR § 300.502]. You are entitled to only one IEE at public expense each time the school district conducts an evaluation with which you disagree. Make your request in writing to the CSE. The school district has the right to set reasonable criteria for IEEs, such as the qualifications of the evaluator.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>


    </div>
</main>
